{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_011CetNiT2c7iRJkEcYu37sf","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Understood. This skill confirms the testing patterns I need to follow. Now let me move to the design phase.\n\n```\n<<PHASE:DESIGN>>\n```\n\n**Proposed change, file by file:**\n\n1. **Order.kt** — Add an `OrderStatus` enum with `ACTIVE` and `CANCELLED` values. Add a `status` field to the `Order` data class with default `ACTIVE`.\n\n2. **OrderController.kt** — Add a new `@PostMapping(\"/{orderId}/cancel\")` endpoint that:\n   - Checks if the order exists (404 if not)\n   - Throws ConflictException (409) if ANY shipment is CONFIRMED\n   - Otherwise: updates order status to CANCELLED, cancels all CREATED shipments, and returns the updated order\n   - Idempotently succeeds if order is already CANCELLED\n\n3. **ApiError.kt** — Add new error codes: `ORDER_CANNOT_CANCEL_CONFIRMED_SHIPMENT` for the 409 conflict case.\n\n4. **ShipmentController.kt** — In the `create` method, add validation to reject shipment creation if the order status is CANCELLED (409 ConflictException).\n\n5. **ShipmentRepository.kt** — Add a `findByOrderId(orderId: String)` method to find all shipments for an order.\n\n6. **OrderControllerTest.kt** — Add comprehensive tests covering:\n   - Cancelling an ACTIVE order succeeds and returns the cancelled order\n   - GET shows the cancelled order status\n   - Cancelling an already CANCELLED order succeeds (idempotency)\n   - Cancelling with a CONFIRMED shipment returns 409 and order/shipments unchanged\n   - Cancelling a non-existent order returns 404\n   - The cancel endpoint cancels CREATED shipments (but not CONFIRMED ones)\n\n7. **ShipmentControllerTest.kt** — Add test for creating a shipment for a CANCELLED order returns 409.\n\n**Alternative considered and rejected:**\n\nI considered creating a separate `OrderService` class to handle the cancel logic, but rejected this because:\n- The existing codebase has no service layer; all logic is in controllers\n- Adding a service layer would be \"unrelated refactoring\" per the boundaries\n- The cancellation logic is simple enough for the controller\n\n**Data and error flow:**\n\n*Successful cancellation of ACTIVE order:*\n1. POST /orders/{orderId}/cancel\n2. OrderController.cancel() retrieves order from repository\n3. If order.status == CANCELLED, return order immediately (idempotent)\n4. If any shipment is CONFIRMED → throw ConflictException (409), nothing changes\n5. Otherwise: set order.status = CANCELLED, find all shipments with CREATED status for this order, set each to CANCELLED, save updated order\n6. Return updated Order with status=CANCELLED\n7. GET /orders/{orderId} shows status=CANCELLED\n\n*Failed cancellation with CONFIRMED shipment:*\n1. POST /orders/{orderId}/cancel\n2. Check shipments, find CONFIRMED one\n3. Throw ConflictException with ORDER_CANNOT_CANCEL_CONFIRMED_SHIPMENT\n4. GlobalExceptionHandler catches it, returns 409 with error envelope\n5. Order and all shipments remain unchanged\n6. GET /orders/{orderId} still shows status=ACTIVE\n\n*Shipment creation for cancelled order:*\n1. POST /shipments with orderId of CANCELLED order\n2. ShipmentController.create() checks order status\n3. Find order is CANCELLED\n4. Throw ConflictException\n5. Return 409 with error envelope\n6. Shipment is not created\n\n**Test strategy:**\n\n1. `cancels an active order and returns the updated order` — Exercise POST /orders/{orderId}/cancel on ACTIVE order, verify response body shows CANCELLED, then GET /orders/{orderId} to confirm persistence\n2. `idempotently succeeds when cancelling already cancelled order` — Cancel once, then cancel again, verify both return 200 with CANCELLED status\n3. `rejects cancellation with 409 if a shipment is confirmed` — Create shipment with CONFIRMED status, attempt cancel, verify 409 with error code, then GET to verify order stays ACTIVE and shipments unchanged\n4. `returns 404 for non-existent order` — Attempt cancel on non-existent ID, verify 404\n5. `cancels only created shipments, not confirmed ones` — Create two shipments, confirm one, cancel order, verify CREATED one is CANCELLED and CONFIRMED one stays CONFIRMED\n6. `rejects shipment creation for cancelled order with 409` — Cancel order, attempt to create shipment, verify 409\n\nNow I'm ready to implement.\n\n```\n<<PHASE:IMPLEMENTATION>>\n```\n\nLet me start by modifying the Order entity to add the status field:"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":9,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1484,"cache_read_input_tokens":25277,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1484},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d2613f5c-ac37-4aa1-821c-330515b9d950","uuid":"3dabf473-b663-4da9-95b7-71a69beab9b1","timestamp":"2026-09-09T18:56:15.781Z","request_id":"req_011CetNiSNuoSAkES9kqoSDL"}
